  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nUpslope rain/snow showers will continue through tonight and \npotentially linger through tomorrow morning as a cold front \nsinks into the area. Weak low pressure riding along that front \nis going to clip our area which will allow for shower activity \nto expand outside of the mountains, but likely not to a high \ndegree. Froude numbers from the RAP and GFS suggest mostly \ncritical flow overnight which would keep precipitation falling \ncloser to the foothills.\n\nAttention then turns to the late week period as low pressure \njogs East with a deep trough emerging off the East Coast, south \nof Long Island, on Thursday night. Any impacts to our area are \ngoing to come from development of a secondary low on Friday also\ndeveloping south of Long Island and here is where models \ncontinue to diverge as the GFS and GEFS ensemble members \ncontinue to have the secondary low get swept into the Gulf of \nMaine, but even so QPF and thusly snow amounts have trended down\nin this scenario. The Euro and Canadian (and their ensembles) \ncontinue to suggest a flatter wave where the secondary low gets \nswept out to sea with little fanfare. Even the RRFS, which keep \nin mind this system is at the tail end of its window, is \nseemingly taking the side of the latter. With all that being \nsaid confidence in exact track and rain/snow amounts is still \nuncertain. However, based on the trends I have seen the past \ncouple days I have growing confidence that this is going to be a\nlow impact event. Significant accumulating snow outside of the \nmountains is looking increasingly less likely, but just about \neveryone still has a good chance of seeing their first flakes of\nthe season (they may just melt on contact). As for the higher \nterrain a couple inches of snow is not out of the question, but \nagain an out to sea track puts this at jeopardy as well.\n\nBelow average temperatures arrive with the aforementioned \nsystem, and remain in its wake. Upslope showers likely linger \nthrough Sunday, but high pressure building in early next week is\ngoing to trend it drier everywhere.",
